// Per-thread CPU migration counts for a specific PID. Microsecond-scale
// benchmarks that refuse to hold tight CV bars are often bouncing between
// cores; this shows it directly (and pinning makes it read zero).
// The bpftrace backend puts the traced process's PID in the filters below.
// The migration filter tracks the main thread -- ideal for single-threaded
// benchmark binaries, where migration is exactly the pinning lesson.
//
// Built entirely on sched-family tracepoints, which every kernel ships --
// including vendor kernels that compile out kprobes and the syscalls
// tracepoint family. No BEGIN/END on purpose: maps auto-print at exit.
//
// args->pid is the migrating thread's id as the host's PID namespace
// numbers it. The trace ends with the process's main thread, whose thread
// id (bpftrace's tid) is the process id.

tracepoint:sched:sched_migrate_task /args->pid == {{PID}}/ {
  @migrations[args->pid] = count();
  @dest_cpu[args->dest_cpu] = count();
}

tracepoint:sched:sched_process_exit /tid == {{PID}}/ {
  exit();
}
